d repaired  re-envisioned and reconstructed  each time laying down a new layer to its  story and some of those layers are  hundreds of years apart  for example the bells you hear ringing  still hang in the two iconic towers of  notre dame de paris  there are 10 in all and each one has a  name  and a story  there's the lowest bell emmanuel which  dates from the 15th century  [Music]  it was melted down and recast in 1681 by  order of louis xiv  the other nine vowels including  anjonville marcel denis were installed  very recently in 2013.  when all of them ring together they  create a soundscape that bridges the  centuries  [Music]  the bells managed to survive the fire in  2019 they rang on the one year  anniversary of the fire and will ring  again in the future  the evolution of this sacred space has  become apparent to tomas during his  assignment he's been covering the story  for more than a year and it has taken  him to the very depths of the  cathedral's past and to its highest  heights quite literally  [Music]  i'm amy briggs executive editor of  national geographic history magazine and  this is overheard at national geographic  a show where we eavesdrop on the wild  conversations we have here at nachio and  follow them to the edges of our big  weird beautiful world  today on overheard we'll hear how a  cathedral is being reborn emerging from  the ashes just as it has done before  and how the legions of people working to  restore it face monumental obstacles to  bring it back to life just as they have  for centuries  more after the break  [Music]  if you think that back in history when  they were building this in the in the  12th century  this cathedral that they were building  must have seemed so huge for the teeny  little village that surrounded it paris  may have been a teeny little village but  in 1163 when construction started it was  one of europe's largest cities at the  time its population was about 40 000  people  the audacity of this project was immense  i mean imagine the construction of a  building that would take almost 200  years to complete  that's the thing that keeps hitting me  and that i can't really get over in the  project is  is just being like fully conscious of  the idea that the people that started  building the cathedral  knew it wouldn't be finished in their  lifetime and i can't really think of  projects that we do today it would say  we're going to start this and we're  going to do it for four generations from  now or five generations from now  when it was finally complete notre dame  would be the tallest structure in paris  for more than 700 years  until the eiffel tower was built in 1889  you can see what an amazing feat it  remains to this day to build a monument  so huge and it feels like it fits the  city now the city is sort of like grown  up to fit notre dum the cathedral stands  both culturally and spiritually at the  very heart of the city and at the heart  of france for that matter since 1769 the  french have called it pois zero which  means that no matter where you are in  france the road signs to paris tell you  how far you are from notre dame it's  kind of this like central vortex of  french history  where so many things have happened  napoleon crowning himself or the french  revolutionaries coming in and blacking  out all the windows and turning into a  temple of reason instead of a church or  the the last uh great reconstruction of  it it's this place that's  that's uh been through the movements of  time and history and yet continues and  it continues because people care about  it people add to it people are inspired  by it so when the fire started on the  evening of april 15 2019  the entire country came to a standstill  hoping that this wouldn't be the end of  their beloved icon  the cathedral was very badly damaged the  iconic spire added in the 19th century  was destroyed and it fell through to the  altar below  two-thirds of the wooden roof was burned  but the organ was saved the stained  glass windows were saved and of course  so were the bells  as a photographer tomas von hutrieff was  uniquely qualified to document this  rebirth  a belgian american who lives in paris  tomas has always used photography as a  way to understand the big picture if  you'll excuse the pun of what it means  to be a part of a place  it's so fascinating to be in this  culture that in many ways is 180 degrees  from my own  and such a wonderful way to enter into  the culture is through uh through a  camera it's kind of like a passport to  get into situations and a lovely pretext  to to show your curiosity and and share  it with people but before tomas could  share his curiosity he had to go on  location and for that he'd need access  little did i know that the access thing  would be one of the most difficult  nuts to crack in my entire photographic  career i would say the top two  of all time since i've been  photographing since 1999 are notre dame  and getting into pyongyang north korea  so just to let you  know  the level of difficulty  so when when maggio called you up it was  like hey do you want to photograph notre  dame  did they mention all of this stuff or  did you learn it after you said yes i  knew  that the access would be a bit trying  uh but i i didn't know how trying it  would be and then as far as the the  safety concerns i didn't know what a big  deal it would be  uh to go through all of this  [Music]  from a technical point of view like  what's the most challenging aspect of it  all the lead poisoning um  mig is what makes the work site very  challenging basically the roof's  covering was made out of lead and the  outer covering of the spire was made out  of lead and so  when it melted basically lead particles  just got everywhere  and and it's toxic for people especially  in large doses and so they set up  extremely strict protocols for entering  the work site had to take a lead  training safety class basically where  you learn  about testing your blood levels for lead  and toxic exposure levels and the best  way to take often is dispose of your  items and then the second thing is  a lot of the work takes place at at  height basically up high either on  scaffolding or hanging on ropes you need  to take a training uh about wearing a  harness and working safely at heights  but eventually with with uh persistence  i got in and it's it's just a whole  other universe in there it's just been  magic ever since then  tomas recalls his first experience in  the cathedral after he had seen it from  the bell tower in the pre-dawn hours  when he finally saw it from the inside  at ground level when you walked in into  notre dame what did you see paint a  picture for me  inside you could look up and see this  gaping hole  up to the sky  with fingers of burnt timber sticking in  at the edges and that is where the spire  had fallen through and just left this  huge gaping hole right in the right in  the very heart of the cathedral so that  that is extremely moving the the first  time you see it because you know if  you've ever been in one of these old  cathedrals just just walking in  your eyes are encouraged by the  architects to look upwards  and then it ends at this devastating  horrible kind of mangled mess of of fire  debris it's very emotional the first  time you go in there and see this  did that initial shock of seeing the  devastation has changed over time for  you  it has because  they're they're cleaning up little by  little now when you walk in  what's shocking is the amount of  scaffolding that is in there there is so  much scaffolding it looks like a space  station or something like that  i guess in any restoration there's you  know some high degree of i don't know  like it's almost like an archaeological  dig what have you witnessed during your  time at notre dame  so  yeah they're like the whole way that  they're treating  this work site is as if it were one huge  archaeological dig it's like you can't  take  one object even if it's completely burnt  and destroyed  off of the cathedral worksite without  sort of tagging it itemizing it  categorizing it  one of the things that struck me as  interesting is that the the stone card  first in the middle ages in order to get  paid would sign their their own  signature or their own mark on the back  of the stones so they would make a  certain style stone that had to fit in a  certain place  the side with the mark is generally put  inside the wall where you can't see it  and so you just see the smooth surface  of these hand-carved stones of course  all these stones have fallen down now  many of them from from the vaulted  ceilings and arches and so you can see  the back side of these stones well now  the scientists and cultural specialists  and historians that are that are working  on it can see okay how many workers did  we have how many different stone carvers  were them  do these same signatures show up on  other cathedrals in europe in germany  and france and so they are able to  reconstruct and peek at information that  they didn't have access to before  oh wow  look there's so many wonderful bits to  touch on there i mean you're getting a  glimpse of the life's work of people who  for the most part largely go  unremembered by history  um and that they're able to trace  their their work throughout europe i  mean that's just that's astounding it's  really amazing  tamas has experienced the cathedral in  ways that probably no one in its long  history ever intended for it to be  experienced from the air  can you talk to me a little bit about  the drones  so  there's there's two aspects to that one  is inside the cathedral especially  when i first arrived before all that  scaffolding was built nobody was allowed  to walk under there because they just  didn't know when it could give out and  so  it was like how could you take pictures  and so i requested permission to fly my  drone in the nave and fly it  next to the nets 