th like  the hazmat suit and bringing all your  your equipment up yeah it's it's mostly  it's mostly the gears that have to be  set up to  document any situation so sometimes i  have multiple lenses with me or if i'm  recording video maybe i need a  microphone with me and so i've gotta  sort of drape this over myself with all  of these carabiners and clips and things  like that but here you're really  climbing on the ropes and attachments  themselves you're not pulling yourself  up you know holding onto a gargoyle's  head or something like that so tell me a  little bit more about the connections  that you were making  a lot of people that are just  comfortable up high and like working  with their body  are attracted to this kind of work so it  just happens that on the team are are a  bunch of climbers so they they do work  where the only access is by rope  basically  the workers that tomas is referring to  are rather  non-traditional  they're called rope access technicians  or cordys in french i mean everything  sounds better on francais nespa  these highly trained climbers are  helping to carry out the incredibly  physical work of restoring the cathedral  and they are doing it on ropes  too to be exact  this aspect of the assignment the  climbing turned out to be a way in for  tomas by working closely with the other  climbers he was able to access not only  parts of the cathedral he was also able  to access a part of the local culture  which can be hard to break through  it's like i had my own cliff in the  middle of paris where i could feel the  thrill of heights and the incredible  panoramic views that you get when you're  high up on something and hang out with  these people that were on the same  wavelength that for me were doing really  interesting and highly technical  specialized work  one of the climbers tomas got to know  was kavin de sal  okay talk about someone who loves the  thrill of heights kaveen had worked on  the eiffel tower nearly 1 000 feet in  t