ed to  tap into the inspiration too  in fact when victor hugo's masterpiece  was published in 1831  it set off a flurry of interest in the  cathedral a massive 20-year restoration  was undertaken in 1844 led by architect  eugene villale duke this was the  restoration that produced the gallery de  chamere the new spire and many of the  iconic features that notre dame became  famous for  for tomas seeing the confluence of all  these elements both intact and in  disrepair became the push and pull of a  journalistic drive to document the  restoration and an artistic drive to  interpret it  so how has notre dam inspired you as an  artist  oh i mean it really in many many ways to  think about the flow of history in our  space and time to think about the amount  of hopes and fears and ideas that have  been projected on this place i have to  say  the strongest uh inspiration is actually  kind of what i described to you when i  first walked in the inside and you're  gazing up and you see the huge hole in  the ceiling i mean  those feelings just go straight through  your soul you know and i was also  inspired by the very beginnings of  photography using a 19th century wet  plate camera and i dragged it all the  way up these stairs inside the north  bell tower and i was able to use that  camera and that also just gave me pause  and said you know look at it in a  different way this camera really forces  you to slow down and also forces you to  channel the way photographers used to  work and used to see the world and so  that was just another of layer that made  this assignment meaningful so as a  photographer you say how can i make a  picture  that somehow  transfers or translates or passes on to  other people this feeling that's moving  through me right now how can i  work the light and the angles and  compositions to to get this feeling you  know out of my heart and in  into the image basically so what are you  hoping people take away from your work  at notre dame i really think that  we we should reflect on this place and  and the role that it's had through time  and culture  and history is kind of one of these few  projects on earth that that brings  people together to make something that's  more than the sum of its parts that  makes something kind of an incredible  human creation sometimes we discover  them  you know forgotten in the jungle  somewhere  great pyramids or things like that but  this is a place like that with the same  kind of amazing collective history  that's still going on and forward  tomas didn't always feel this way about  notre dame as a longtime resident of  paris he used to avoid it and the  upwards of 30 000 tourists that it used  to attract each day i would see notre  dame and and want to go to it and then  say oh there's just so many people and  there's somebody selling postcards and  water bottles and this huge line of  people and just say forget it so this is  kind of like a really  rare occasion where i can be a witness  in a place that a lot of people know and  care about  but very few people have access to at  the moment  [Music]  the scale of this cathedral  of time and history can be hard to  fathom  tomas has become so fully steeped in its  many layers that as he points out he has  become a layer himself a hundred years  from now there'll be a photographer like  me that's looking up for old photos of  the last reconstruction of notre dame  well maybe my photos will be stumbled  upon there and they'll have a have a  second life in that sense too  the devastating fire of 2019 may have  added one more layer to the cathedral's  ongoing evolution  but it won't be the last one  as so many who have dedicated themselves  to this building have learned they are a  part of its very long story  [Music]  i'm just happy to be part of notre  dame's incredible story and trajectory  going through history even if i'm a  little part of it  [Music]  if you like what you hear and want to  support more content like this pl